INSERT INTO `services` (`id`, `name`, `thumbnail`, `duration`, `price`, `description`, `clinic_id`, `speciality_id`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3, 'General Health Checkup', 'clinic_image_6802895934c2e.webp', 90, 650, '<p>\n\n<p data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"371\">A <strong data-start=\"2\" data-end=\"28\">General Health Checkup</strong> is a comprehensive and preventative medical examination aimed at assessing your overall health and identifying any potential health issues before they become serious. This routine checkup is designed for individuals of all ages and is crucial for maintaining good health by detecting early signs of illnesses that may otherwise go unnoticed.</p>\n<p data-start=\"373\" data-end=\"893\">During the checkup, you will undergo a series of tests and screenings that typically include a physical examination, blood tests, urine tests, and imaging studies such as an ultrasound or chest X-ray. These tests help evaluate key health markers like blood pressure, cholesterol levels, blood sugar levels, and kidney and liver function. Additionally, the checkup may include checks for heart health through an ECG (electrocardiogram), as well as a general assessment of lung function, weight, and body mass index (BMI).</p><p data-start=\"373\" data-end=\"893\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><img style=\"max-width: 80%; height: 294px;\" src=\"http://127.0.0.1:8000/storage/ServiceContent/service_content_6802898b1532b.webp\" /></p>\n<p data-start=\"895\" data-end=\"1246\">The goal of a general health checkup is not only to detect underlying medical conditions but also to provide health advice and lifestyle recommendations to improve your long-term well-being. This service can be an essential part of a healthy lifestyle, enabling early intervention and providing you with a detailed understanding of your health status.</p>\n<p data-start=\"1248\" data-end=\"1424\">By scheduling regular general health checkups, you are taking an active role in preventing diseases, managing risk factors, and ensuring that you live a healthier, longer life.</p>\n<br /></p>', 1, 9, '2025-04-18 17:19:37', '2025-04-18 17:19:37'),
(4, 'Men\'s Health Checkup', 'clinic_image_68028a214c25a.webp', 60, 450, '<p>\n\n<p data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"376\">A <strong data-start=\"2\" data-end=\"26\">Men\'s Health Checkup</strong> is a specialized medical evaluation designed to assess the health and well-being of men, focusing on issues that are specific to male health. This checkup typically involves a series of diagnostic tests and screenings aimed at identifying common health concerns and preventing chronic conditions that are prevalent among men, especially as they age.</p><p data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"376\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><img style=\"max-width: 80%; height: 209px;\" src=\"http://127.0.0.1:8000/storage/ServiceContent/service_content_68028acbd374f.webp\" /></p>\n<p data-start=\"378\" data-end=\"837\">During the checkup, the doctor will perform a thorough physical examination and may check vital signs such as blood pressure, heart rate, and respiratory rate. Key tests may include blood tests to assess cholesterol levels, liver and kidney function, and glucose levels, as well as prostate-specific antigen (PSA) tests to screen for potential prostate issues. Urine tests are often conducted to check for signs of kidney problems or urinary tract infections.</p>\n<p data-start=\"839\" data-end=\"1188\">A significant component of a Men\'s Health Checkup is screening for cardiovascular disease, which is a leading cause of death among men. This may involve an ECG (electrocardiogram), stress tests, and cholesterol screenings. Other common tests include checking for signs of diabetes, liver diseases, and certain sexually transmitted infections (STIs).</p><p data-start=\"839\" data-end=\"1188\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><img style=\"max-width: 80%; height: 221px;\" src=\"http://127.0.0.1:8000/storage/ServiceContent/service_content_68028afbe6c52.webp\" /></p>\n<p data-start=\"1190\" data-end=\"1497\">For men in their middle and later years, the checkup may also include a prostate ultrasound and an evaluation of sexual health, including testosterone levels, erectile function, and a general review of mental health concerns like stress and depression, which are often overlooked in male health assessments.</p>\n<p data-start=\"1499\" data-end=\"1861\">Overall, a Men\'s Health Checkup serves as an important tool in identifying health issues early, allowing for timely interventions that can significantly improve quality of life, prevent major health issues, and promote long-term health and wellness. Regular checkups are essential for maintaining an active, healthy lifestyle and preventing age-related diseases.</p>\n<br /></p>', 1, 8, '2025-04-18 17:25:34', '2025-04-18 17:26:13'),
(5, 'Cardiology Checkup', 'clinic_image_68028bab56cae.webp', 60, 100, '<p>\n\n</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"468\">A <strong data-start=\"2\" data-end=\"24\">Cardiology Checkup</strong> is a specialized health assessment focused on evaluating the health of the heart and cardiovascular system. This checkup is essential for individuals who have risk factors for heart disease, such as a family history of heart conditions, high blood pressure, diabetes, high cholesterol, smoking, or a sedentary lifestyle. It is also crucial for individuals experiencing symptoms such as chest pain, shortness of breath, or irregular heartbeats.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"468\"><br /></p>\r\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img style=\"max-width: 80%;\" src=\"http://127.0.0.1:8000/storage/ServiceContent/service_content_68028c783fb1a.webp\" /></div>\r\n\n\r\n<p data-start=\"470\" data-end=\"703\">During the checkup, the healthcare provider typically begins with a thorough physical examination, including an evaluation of blood pressure, heart rate, and respiration. Key tests often performed during a cardiology checkup include:</p>\r\n\n\r\n<ol><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"708\" data-end=\"742\">Electrocardiogram (ECG or EKG)</strong>: This test measures the electrical activity of the heart and can help detect irregularities in heart rhythm, previous heart attacks, or other heart conditions.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"909\" data-end=\"927\">Echocardiogram</strong>: A non-invasive ultrasound test that uses sound waves to create images of the heart, allowing the doctor to evaluate the heart\'s size, structure, and function.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1092\" data-end=\"1107\">Stress Test</strong>: This test involves exercising on a treadmill or stationary bike while being monitored to assess how the heart responds to physical exertion. It helps identify potential heart problems that may not be apparent when the body is at rest.\n<br /></li><li><span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Blood Tests</span>: These tests can provide important information about cholesterol levels, triglycerides, blood sugar levels, and markers that may indicate heart disease, such as high levels of LDL (bad cholesterol) or C-reactive protein (CRP), which is an indicator of inflammation.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1634\" data-end=\"1664\">Blood Pressure Measurement</strong>: Consistently high blood pressure can increase the risk of heart disease, so monitoring blood pressure is a critical component of a cardiology checkup.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1821\" data-end=\"1843\">X-rays or CT Scans</strong>: In some cases, imaging tests may be ordered to visualize the heart and blood vessels, helping detect problems such as artery blockages or heart enlargement.\n<br /></li></ol>\r\n<div>\n\n<p data-start=\"2003\" data-end=\"2358\">A <strong data-start=\"2005\" data-end=\"2027\">Cardiology Checkup</strong> can help detect early signs of heart disease, such as coronary artery disease, heart valve problems, arrhythmias, or heart failure, allowing for early intervention and treatment. It also provides an opportunity to assess and manage risk factors, such as high cholesterol or hypertension, to prevent future heart-related problems.</p>\n<p data-start=\"2360\" data-end=\"2641\">Regular checkups are important for maintaining heart health, especially as individuals age or develop risk factors, ensuring that any potential issues are identified and addressed promptly to reduce the likelihood of serious cardiovascular events, such as heart attacks or strokes.</p>\n<br /></div>', 1, 2, '2025-04-18 17:31:43', '2025-04-18 17:31:43'),
(6, 'Pediatric Checkup', 'clinic_image_68028cb113596.webp', 45, 50, '<p>\n\nA <strong data-start=\"2\" data-end=\"23\">Pediatric Checkup</strong> is a comprehensive health evaluation designed specifically for children, from infants to adolescents. These checkups are essential for monitoring a child’s growth, development, and overall health, as well as for preventing or detecting any potential health issues early on. Regular pediatric checkups help ensure that children are on track with their physical, emotional, and mental development.\n<br /></p>\r\n<p>\n\nDuring the checkup, the pediatrician will perform a thorough physical examination, assessing the child’s overall health, growth, and development. Key components of a pediatric checkup typically include:\n<br /></p>\r\n<p><ol><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"628\" data-end=\"649\">Growth Monitoring</strong>: The doctor will track the child’s height, weight, and body mass index (BMI) to ensure that they are growing at a healthy rate. Growth charts are used to compare the child’s development with standard growth patterns for their age.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"885\" data-end=\"914\">Developmental Assessments</strong>: The pediatrician will evaluate the child’s physical, cognitive, and emotional development. This may include checking milestones such as motor skills, language skills, social interaction, and behavior. The doctor will discuss any concerns regarding developmental delays or behavioral issues.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1211\" data-end=\"1227\">Vaccinations</strong>: Pediatric checkups are also a key time to ensure that the child is up-to-date on required immunizations. The pediatrician will review the child’s vaccination history and administer any necessary shots to protect against diseases such as measles, mumps, rubella, polio, and influenza.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1517\" data-end=\"1545\">Vision and Hearing Tests</strong>: Regular screening for vision and hearing problems is an important part of pediatric checkups. Early detection of vision or hearing impairments can prevent long-term developmental issues.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1738\" data-end=\"1763\">Physical Examinations</strong>: The pediatrician will perform a physical exam, checking the child’s eyes, ears, throat, lungs, heart, abdomen, and musculoskeletal system. They will listen for any abnormal heartbeats or lung sounds, check for signs of infection, and ensure that the child’s organs are functioning properly.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2060\" data-end=\"2101\">Mental and Emotional Health Screening</strong>: Mental health is a crucial aspect of a child’s overall well-being. Pediatricians may ask questions about the child’s mood, behavior, sleep patterns, and any signs of anxiety or depression. Early identification of mental health issues can lead to effective intervention and support.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2389\" data-end=\"2419\">Blood Tests and Screenings</strong>: Depending on the child’s age and health history, certain blood tests may be recommended to check for conditions like anemia, lead poisoning, cholesterol levels, and other health concerns.\n<br /></li><li><span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Nutritional Guidance</span>: The pediatrician may discuss the child’s diet and nutrition, offering guidance on healthy eating habits to support growth and development. They may provide advice on portion sizes, balanced meals, and healthy snacks.\n<br /></li></ol><div>\n\nA <strong data-start=\"2859\" data-end=\"2880\">Pediatric Checkup</strong> is an essential part of preventive healthcare for children, helping to identify any potential health concerns early on, provide vaccinations to prevent diseases, and ensure that the child is developing in a healthy and timely manner. By maintaining regular checkups, parents can ensure their child stays healthy, both physically and emotionally, and receives the appropriate care at every stage of their growth.\n<br /></div></p>', 1, 4, '2025-04-18 17:36:59', '2025-04-18 17:36:59'),
(7, 'Endocrinology Checkup', 'clinic_image_68028df872de7.webp', 45, 100, '<p>\n\n</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"527\">An <strong data-start=\"3\" data-end=\"28\">Endocrinology Checkup</strong> is a specialized medical evaluation focused on the diagnosis, treatment, and prevention of hormone-related disorders. The endocrine system regulates vital bodily functions through the secretion of hormones by various glands, including the thyroid, pancreas, adrenal glands, and reproductive organs. An endocrinology checkup is essential for individuals experiencing symptoms that may indicate hormonal imbalances, such as unexplained weight changes, fatigue, excessive thirst, or irregular periods.</p>\r\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img style=\"max-width: 80%; height: 407px;\" src=\"http://127.0.0.1:8000/storage/ServiceContent/service_content_68028e990c91a.webp\" /></div>\r\n\n\r\n<p data-start=\"529\" data-end=\"723\">During an <strong data-start=\"539\" data-end=\"564\">Endocrinology Checkup</strong>, the healthcare provider will typically begin with a detailed medical history and physical examination. Common components of an endocrinology checkup include:</p>\r\n\n\r\n<ol><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"728\" data-end=\"756\">Thyroid Function Testing</strong>: The thyroid is responsible for regulating metabolism and energy levels. Blood tests are used to measure levels of thyroid hormones such as TSH (Thyroid Stimulating Hormone), T3, and T4. These tests help diagnose conditions like hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid) or hyperthyroidism (overactive thyroid).\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1068\" data-end=\"1093\">Blood Glucose Testing</strong>: One of the most common endocrine conditions is diabetes, which involves an imbalance in insulin production and blood sugar regulation. A blood test to measure fasting blood glucose or HbA1c levels can help diagnose or monitor diabetes, prediabetes, or insulin resistance.\n<br /></li><li><span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Hormone Level Testing</span>: Depending on the patient\'s symptoms, blood tests may be ordered to check for imbalances in other hormones, including cortisol (the stress hormone), estrogen, testosterone, and progesterone. These tests can help identify conditions like adrenal insufficiency, polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), or infertility issues.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1718\" data-end=\"1752\">Adrenal Gland Function Testing</strong>: The adrenal glands produce hormones like adrenaline, cortisol, and aldosterone. Blood and urine tests may be conducted to evaluate adrenal function and diagnose conditions such as Addison\'s disease or Cushing\'s syndrome, which result from imbalances in adrenal hormones.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2029\" data-end=\"2053\">Bone Density Testing</strong>: Hormonal imbalances, particularly those related to the thyroid or sex hormones, can affect bone health. A bone density scan may be recommended to check for osteoporosis or bone thinning, conditions often associated with hormonal changes, especially during menopause or in individuals with thyroid disorders.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2367\" data-end=\"2401\">Reproductive Health Evaluation</strong>: For individuals experiencing irregular periods, infertility, or symptoms related to reproductive health, an endocrinology checkup may include hormone testing to assess levels of estrogen, progesterone, and prolactin. These tests help diagnose issues like polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), premature ovarian failure, or low sperm count in men.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2750\" data-end=\"2774\">Physical Examination</strong>: The doctor may assess physical signs of hormonal imbalances, such as changes in skin texture, excessive body hair, or unusual fat distribution. These signs can help identify conditions like thyroid disorders, PCOS, or hypercortisolism (Cushing\'s syndrome).\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"3037\" data-end=\"3054\">Imaging Tests</strong>: In some cases, the endocrinologist may recommend imaging tests such as ultrasounds, CT scans, or MRIs to assess the size and function of endocrine glands like the thyroid, pituitary, or adrenal glands.\n<br /></li></ol>\r\n<div>\n\nAn <strong data-start=\"3262\" data-end=\"3287\">Endocrinology Checkup</strong> is vital for diagnosing and managing a range of endocrine disorders, including diabetes, thyroid disorders, adrenal disorders, and reproductive issues. Early detection of hormonal imbalances can lead to more effective treatments, such as medication or hormone replacement therapy, and can significantly improve overall health and quality of life. Regular checkups are especially important for individuals with a family history of endocrine disorders or those experiencing symptoms related to hormonal imbalances.\n<br /></div>', 1, 6, '2025-04-18 17:40:52', '2025-04-18 17:40:52'),
(8, 'Pulmonary Checkup', 'clinic_image_68028ec0a02c2.webp', 40, 90, '<p>\n\n<p data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"567\">A <strong data-start=\"2\" data-end=\"23\">Pulmonary Checkup</strong> is a specialized health assessment focused on evaluating the function and health of the lungs and respiratory system. This checkup is essential for individuals who are at risk of respiratory diseases, such as smokers, individuals with a family history of lung conditions, or those who experience symptoms like persistent cough, shortness of breath, wheezing, or chest pain. Pulmonary checkups are particularly important for the early detection of conditions like asthma, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), pneumonia, or lung cancer.</p><p data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"567\"><figure style=\"display: inline-block; text-align: center;\"><img style=\"max-width: 80%;\" src=\"http://127.0.0.1:8000/storage/ServiceContent/service_content_68028fb19331b.webp\" /><figcaption></figcaption></figure></p>\n<p data-start=\"569\" data-end=\"794\">During a <strong data-start=\"578\" data-end=\"599\">Pulmonary Checkup</strong>, the healthcare provider will conduct a series of tests and assessments to evaluate lung function and identify any potential respiratory issues. Common components of a pulmonary checkup include:</p>\n<ol><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"799\" data-end=\"823\">Physical Examination</strong>: The doctor will listen to the lungs using a stethoscope to detect abnormal breath sounds such as wheezing, crackles, or rhonchi, which may indicate respiratory problems. The provider will also check for signs of labored breathing, cyanosis (bluish skin color due to lack of oxygen), or abnormal chest shape.\n<br /></li><li><span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Spirometry (Pulmonary Function Test)</span>: This is one of the most common tests in a pulmonary checkup. Spirometry measures the amount and speed of air you can exhale after taking a deep breath. It helps assess lung function and diagnose conditions like asthma, chronic bronchitis, and emphysema. The results help the doctor evaluate how effectively the lungs are working.\n<br /></li><li><span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Chest X-ray</span>: A chest X-ray provides a detailed image of the lungs, heart, and chest cavity. It can help detect conditions such as lung infections, tumors, or structural problems in the lungs and airways. This imaging test is commonly used to screen for lung cancer, pneumonia, or chronic lung diseases.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1824\" data-end=\"1858\">Blood Oxygen Level Measurement</strong>: The doctor may use a pulse oximeter, a small device clipped to your finger, to measure the level of oxygen in your blood. Low oxygen levels can be a sign of respiratory issues such as COPD or pulmonary fibrosis, where the lungs are unable to effectively exchange oxygen and carbon dioxide.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2154\" data-end=\"2187\">Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) Test</strong>: This test measures the levels of oxygen and carbon dioxide in your blood and can help assess how well your lungs are delivering oxygen to the bloodstream and removing carbon dioxide. It is especially useful for diagnosing severe lung diseases.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2437\" data-end=\"2461\">CT Scan of the Chest</strong>: A high-resolution CT scan may be ordered for more detailed images of the lungs, especially if there are abnormalities found in the chest X-ray. A CT scan is commonly used to evaluate conditions like lung cancer, pulmonary embolism, or interstitial lung disease.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2729\" data-end=\"2745\">Bronchoscopy</strong>: If necessary, a bronchoscopy may be performed. During this procedure, a thin tube with a camera is inserted through the nose or mouth into the lungs to directly view the airways. This is used to diagnose lung diseases, collect tissue samples, or clear blocked airways.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"3020\" data-end=\"3039\">Allergy Testing</strong>: Since respiratory issues like asthma can be triggered by allergies, an allergy test may be included in a pulmonary checkup. The test helps identify allergens such as pollen, dust, mold, or pet dander that may be contributing to respiratory symptoms.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"3295\" data-end=\"3324\">Exhaled Nitric Oxide Test</strong>: This test measures the level of nitric oxide in your breath, which can help assess inflammation in the airways, a common feature of asthma and other respiratory conditions. Elevated levels of nitric oxide may indicate poor asthma control or inflammation in the airways.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"3601\" data-end=\"3616\">Sputum Test</strong>: If a patient is coughing up mucus (sputum), a sputum test may be conducted to check for infections or conditions such as tuberculosis (TB) or bacterial pneumonia. The sample is examined under a microscope to identify any harmful microorganisms.\n<br /></li></ol><div>\n\nA <strong data-start=\"3866\" data-end=\"3887\">Pulmonary Checkup</strong> is vital for diagnosing and managing a range of respiratory conditions, from asthma and COPD to more serious conditions like lung cancer or pulmonary fibrosis. Early detection and intervention can significantly improve lung health, help manage symptoms, and prevent further complications. Regular pulmonary checkups are especially important for individuals with risk factors like smoking, chronic respiratory symptoms, or a family history of lung diseases.\n<br /></div></p>', 1, 7, '2025-04-18 17:46:00', '2025-04-18 17:46:00'),
(9, 'Neurology Checkup', 'clinic_image_6802902a11964.webp', 70, 120, '<p>\n\n</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"549\">A <strong data-start=\"2\" data-end=\"23\">Neurology Checkup</strong> is a comprehensive medical evaluation focused on assessing the health of the nervous system, which includes the brain, spinal cord, and peripheral nerves. This checkup is essential for individuals experiencing neurological symptoms such as headaches, dizziness, memory problems, weakness, numbness, tremors, seizures, or difficulty with coordination. Neurological disorders, such as Alzheimer\'s disease, Parkinson\'s disease, epilepsy, multiple sclerosis, and stroke, can often be managed more effectively when detected early.</p>\r\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img style=\"max-width: 80%;\" src=\"http://127.0.0.1:8000/storage/ServiceContent/service_content_6802911faa9dd.webp\" /></div>\r\n\n\r\n<p data-start=\"551\" data-end=\"750\">During a <strong data-start=\"560\" data-end=\"581\">Neurology Checkup</strong>, the healthcare provider will conduct a thorough assessment to evaluate the function of the nervous system. The key components of a neurology checkup typically include:</p>\r\n\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<ol><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"755\" data-end=\"793\">Medical History and Symptom Review</strong>: The doctor will begin by asking about any symptoms you are experiencing, your medical history, family history of neurological conditions, and any relevant lifestyle factors. This helps identify potential causes of neurological symptoms.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1036\" data-end=\"1077\">Physical and Neurological Examination</strong>: The neurologist will perform a comprehensive physical exam, checking for signs of neurological problems. This often includes testing reflexes, coordination, balance, muscle strength, and sensory responses. The doctor may ask you to walk, touch your nose, or perform other tasks to assess coordination and motor control.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1403\" data-end=\"1431\">Cognitive Function Tests</strong>: Cognitive tests are used to assess memory, attention, language, and problem-solving skills. These tests help evaluate brain function and can assist in diagnosing conditions like dementia, Alzheimer\'s disease, or other cognitive disorders.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1676\" data-end=\"1691\">Blood Tests</strong>: Blood tests may be ordered to rule out conditions that can affect neurological health, such as infections, autoimmune diseases, or vitamin deficiencies. For example, low levels of vitamin B12 or thyroid problems can contribute to neurological symptoms like numbness or memory loss.\n<br /></li><li><strong data-start=\"2518\" data-end=\"2548\">Electroencephalogram (EEG)</strong>: An EEG is used to measure electrical activity in the brain. It is often used to diagnose conditions like epilepsy or seizures by detecting unusual patterns of brain waves. An EEG can also help evaluate sleep disorders and other neurological conditions.</li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2807\" data-end=\"2868\">Nerve Conduction Studies (NCS) and Electromyography (EMG)</strong>: These tests assess the function of the peripheral nerves and muscles. NCS measures how well electrical impulses travel through the nerves, while EMG evaluates the electrical activity in the muscles. These tests are used to diagnose conditions like neuropathy, carpal tunnel syndrome, or muscle disorders.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"3179\" data-end=\"3211\">Lumbar Puncture (Spinal Tap)</strong>: In some cases, a lumbar puncture may be performed to collect cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) from the spinal canal. This test can help diagnose infections like meningitis, multiple sclerosis, or conditions affecting the brain and spinal cord.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"3454\" data-end=\"3480\">Stroke Risk Assessment</strong>: For individuals at risk of stroke, the neurologist may evaluate blood flow to the brain through imaging tests like a carotid ultrasound, which can detect blockages in the arteries that supply blood to the brain.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"3699\" data-end=\"3718\">Genetic Testing</strong>: In some cases, genetic tests may be recommended to identify hereditary neurological conditions, such as Huntington\'s disease or familial Alzheimer\'s disease.\n<br /></li></ol>\r\n<div><br /></div>\r\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img style=\"max-width: 80%; height: 302px;\" src=\"http://127.0.0.1:8000/storage/ServiceContent/service_content_68029130a253e.webp\" /></div>\r\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><br /></p>\r\n<div>\n\nA <strong data-start=\"3881\" data-end=\"3902\">Neurology Checkup</strong> is crucial for diagnosing and managing a variety of neurological disorders. Early detection of conditions like dementia, epilepsy, or Parkinson\'s disease can lead to more effective treatment and better long-term outcomes. Regular checkups are especially important for individuals with a family history of neurological diseases or those experiencing persistent symptoms that may indicate a neurological condition. By seeking early evaluation, individuals can receive the necessary care to manage symptoms, improve quality of life, and prevent further complications.\n<br /></div>', 1, 3, '2025-04-18 17:52:04', '2025-04-18 17:52:04'),
(10, 'Psychiatric Evaluation', 'clinic_image_6802919c46afa.webp', 45, 90, '<p>\n\n</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"539\">A <strong data-start=\"2\" data-end=\"28\">Psychiatric Evaluation</strong> is a comprehensive assessment conducted by a mental health professional, such as a psychiatrist, psychologist, or clinical social worker, to diagnose and understand a person\'s mental health condition. This evaluation is crucial for individuals experiencing symptoms such as anxiety, depression, mood swings, paranoia, hallucinations, or difficulty coping with life stressors. The goal of the evaluation is to identify mental health disorders, understand their causes, and develop an appropriate treatment plan.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"539\"><br /></p>\r\n\n\r\n<p data-start=\"541\" data-end=\"698\">During a <strong data-start=\"550\" data-end=\"576\">Psychiatric Evaluation</strong>, the mental health professional will typically conduct a thorough assessment, which may include the following components:</p>\r\n\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img style=\"max-width: 80%;\" src=\"http://127.0.0.1:8000/storage/ServiceContent/service_content_68029226b85d2.webp\" /></div>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<ol><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"703\" data-end=\"724\">Initial Interview</strong>: The clinician will begin by asking the individual to describe their symptoms, history of mental health issues, family history of mental illness, and any past treatments or medications. The clinician may also inquire about personal history, including significant life events, trauma, and relationships.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1032\" data-end=\"1067\">Mental Status Examination (MSE)</strong>: The mental status examination is a structured assessment of the individual\'s current mental state. The clinician will observe and ask questions to assess the person\'s appearance, behavior, mood, speech, thought processes, thought content, memory, and cognition. The goal is to evaluate how the person is thinking, feeling, and functioning in their current state.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1436\" data-end=\"1454\">Symptom Review</strong>: The clinician will ask specific questions about symptoms related to mental health disorders. For example, they may ask about mood changes, sleep patterns, appetite, energy levels, concentration, and social withdrawal. They may also inquire about thoughts of self-harm, suicide, or harming others.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1757\" data-end=\"1797\">Psychological Testing (if necessary)</strong>: In some cases, psychological testing or standardized questionnaires may be used to assess specific mental health conditions, such as depression, anxiety, or personality disorders. Tests such as the Beck Depression Inventory (BDI) or the Generalized Anxiety Disorder 7 (GAD-7) can provide additional insight into the severity and nature of symptoms.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2152\" data-end=\"2178\">Physical Health Review</strong>: Since certain physical health conditions, medications, or substance use can contribute to psychiatric symptoms, the clinician may also inquire about the person\'s overall health, medications, and any history of substance abuse. This helps to rule out medical causes for the symptoms or ensure that physical health issues are addressed alongside mental health care.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2548\" data-end=\"2567\">Risk Assessment</strong>: The clinician will assess the individual’s level of risk, particularly with regard to suicidal ideation, self-harm, or violent behavior. If the individual is at risk of harm, appropriate measures will be taken to ensure safety, which may include hospitalization or emergency intervention.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2862\" data-end=\"2875\">Diagnosis</strong>: Based on the information gathered during the evaluation, the mental health professional will use established diagnostic criteria (such as the DSM-5, the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders) to make a diagnosis. This could range from mood disorders like depression and bipolar disorder to anxiety disorders, psychotic disorders, personality disorders, or substance use disorders.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"3278\" data-end=\"3296\">Treatment Plan</strong>: After the evaluation, the mental health professional will discuss potential treatment options. Treatment may include psychotherapy (such as cognitive-behavioral therapy or psychodynamic therapy), medication (such as antidepressants, mood stabilizers, or antipsychotics), lifestyle changes, or a combination of these approaches. The treatment plan will be tailored to the individual’s needs and goals.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"3703\" data-end=\"3728\">Follow-up and Support</strong>: A psychiatric evaluation may lead to ongoing therapy or support, with follow-up appointments scheduled to monitor progress, adjust treatment plans, and ensure the person is responding well to treatment. Support networks, such as family or community resources, may also be part of the treatment process.\n<br /></li></ol>\r\n<div><br /></div>\r\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img style=\"max-width: 80%;\" src=\"http://127.0.0.1:8000/storage/ServiceContent/service_content_6802923975b93.webp\" /></div>\r\n<p>\n\nA <strong data-start=\"4036\" data-end=\"4062\">Psychiatric Evaluation</strong> is an essential tool for diagnosing and managing mental health conditions. Early identification and treatment can significantly improve an individual\'s quality of life and help prevent more severe symptoms or complications. Whether for short-term issues or long-term conditions, a psychiatric evaluation provides a foundation for effective treatment, helping individuals regain control of their mental well-being and live a more fulfilling life.\n</p>', 1, 5, '2025-04-18 17:56:23', '2025-04-18 17:56:23'),
(11, 'Orthopedic Checkup', 'clinic_image_68029276b2e8d.webp', 60, 100, '<p>\n\nAn <strong data-start=\"3\" data-end=\"25\">Orthopedic Checkup</strong> is a medical evaluation focused on diagnosing and treating disorders related to the musculoskeletal system, which includes the bones, joints, muscles, tendons, and ligaments. This checkup is important for individuals who experience musculoskeletal pain, stiffness, mobility issues, or injuries. It is also recommended for those with a history of bone and joint problems or those who are at risk due to factors like age, physical activity, or occupation.</p>\r\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img style=\"max-width: 80%;\" src=\"http://127.0.0.1:8000/storage/ServiceContent/service_content_680293408608d.webp\" /></div>\r\n<p>\n\nDuring an \n<span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Orthopedic Checkup</span>, the orthopedic specialist will conduct a thorough assessment to evaluate the health of your bones, joints, and muscles. Key components of an orthopedic checkup typically include:</p>\r\n<ol><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"698\" data-end=\"736\">Medical History and Symptom Review</strong><span>: The doctor will begin by asking about the symptoms you are experiencing, such as pain, swelling, stiffness, or instability. They will inquire about the duration and intensity of these symptoms, any history of previous injuries or surgeries, and any underlying conditions such as arthritis, osteoporosis, or genetic bone disorders.</span>\n<br /></li><li><span>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1073\" data-end=\"1097\">Physical Examination</strong>: The orthopedic specialist will perform a physical examination to assess the range of motion, strength, and stability of your joints and muscles. They may ask you to move specific joints or limbs in different directions to evaluate flexibility and detect any signs of pain or abnormal motion.\n<br /></span></li><li><span><span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Posture and Gait Analysis</span>: The doctor may observe your posture and walking pattern (gait) to identify any abnormalities that could indicate musculoskeletal problems. This is especially useful for detecting issues with the spine, hips, knees, or feet.\n<br /></span></li><li><span>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1654\" data-end=\"1681\">X-rays or Imaging Tests</strong>: Imaging tests, such as X-rays, are commonly used to visualize the bones and joints. These tests can help identify fractures, joint degeneration, bone spurs, or abnormalities in bone alignment. If further detailed imaging is required, the doctor may recommend a <strong data-start=\"1944\" data-end=\"1955\">CT scan</strong> or <strong data-start=\"1959\" data-end=\"1966\">MRI</strong> to assess the soft tissues, cartilage, tendons, and ligaments surrounding the joints.\n<br /></span></li><li><span>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2057\" data-end=\"2078\">Bone Density Test</strong>: If you are at risk for osteoporosis (bone thinning), the orthopedic specialist may recommend a bone density test (DEXA scan) to measure the strength of your bones and assess the risk of fractures.\n<br /></span></li><li><span>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2281\" data-end=\"2317\">Joint and Soft Tissue Ultrasound</strong>: In some cases, an ultrasound may be used to evaluate the soft tissues around joints, such as tendons, ligaments, and muscles. This test is non-invasive and can help diagnose conditions like tendonitis, bursitis, or ligament injuries.\n<br /></span></li><li><span>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2557\" data-end=\"2572\">Blood Tests</strong>: If an inflammatory or autoimmune condition, such as rheumatoid arthritis, is suspected, the orthopedic specialist may order blood tests to measure inflammatory markers (like C-reactive protein or rheumatoid factor) or assess levels of specific antibodies.\n<br /></span></li><li><span>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2834\" data-end=\"2869\">Functional and Strength Testing</strong>: Depending on the symptoms, the orthopedic specialist may assess muscle strength, balance, and the ability to perform basic movements. This can help identify areas of weakness or dysfunction that may contribute to pain or limited mobility.\n<br /></span></li><li><span>\n\n<strong data-start=\"3114\" data-end=\"3143\">Assessment of Bone Health</strong>: For older adults or individuals at risk of fractures, the doctor may evaluate bone health and recommend lifestyle changes or treatments to strengthen bones, such as weight-bearing exercises, calcium, and vitamin D supplementation.\n<br /></span></li><li><span>\n\n<strong data-start=\"3381\" data-end=\"3399\">Treatment Plan</strong>: Based on the findings of the evaluation, the orthopedic specialist will discuss potential treatment options. This may include physical therapy, medications (such as pain relievers or anti-inflammatory drugs), lifestyle modifications (such as weight management or exercise), or surgical intervention if necessary.\n<br /></span></li></ol>\r\n<div>\n\nA <strong data-start=\"3717\" data-end=\"3753\">Comprehensive Orthopedic Checkup</strong> is crucial for maintaining bone and joint health, identifying underlying musculoskeletal conditions, and preventing future injuries. Whether you are experiencing chronic pain, recovering from an injury, or concerned about bone health, an orthopedic checkup helps to diagnose the issue and develop an appropriate treatment plan. Early intervention can improve mobility, reduce pain, and enhance your quality of life. Regular checkups are especially important for individuals at risk for musculoskeletal disorders, such as older adults, athletes, or those with a family history of bone diseases.\n<br /></div>', 1, 1, '2025-04-18 18:00:42', '2025-04-18 18:00:42'),
(12, 'Dental Checkup', 'clinic_image_68029536a5c83.webp', 60, 40, '<p>\n\nA <strong data-start=\"2\" data-end=\"20\">Dental Checkup</strong> is a routine visit to the dentist designed to assess the health of your teeth, gums, and mouth. It is an essential part of preventive oral care, helping to detect dental problems early, such as cavities, gum disease, or oral cancer, before they become serious. Regular dental checkups are important for maintaining good oral hygiene, preventing tooth decay, and ensuring the overall health of your teeth and gums.\n<br /></p>\r\n<p>\n\nDuring a <strong data-start=\"445\" data-end=\"463\">Dental Checkup</strong>, the dentist or dental hygienist will typically perform the following procedures:\n<br /></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<ol><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"550\" data-end=\"587\">Medical and Dental History Review</strong>: The dentist will ask about your medical history, including any medications you are taking, allergies, or health conditions that may affect your oral health. They will also inquire about any recent dental concerns or changes in your oral health, such as pain, sensitivity, or bleeding gums.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"883\" data-end=\"907\">Physical Examination</strong>: The dentist will examine your mouth, teeth, and gums for signs of dental issues, such as cavities, plaque buildup, or gum disease. They will check for signs of inflammation, infection, or oral cancer by inspecting the soft tissues of the mouth, tongue, and throat.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1178\" data-end=\"1220\">Teeth Cleaning (Scaling and Polishing)</strong>: A dental hygienist will typically clean your teeth to remove plaque and tartar (hardened plaque) buildup. This is done using specialized dental tools, such as an ultrasonic scaler and hand scalers. Cleaning helps prevent gum disease, tooth decay, and bad breath by removing the bacteria that accumulate on the teeth and gums.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1552\" data-end=\"1576\">X-rays (Radiographs)</strong>: Depending on the evaluation, the dentist may recommend X-rays to check for issues that are not visible during a physical examination, such as cavities between the teeth, impacted teeth, bone loss, or infections at the root of the tooth. Dental X-rays help the dentist identify problems early, allowing for timely treatment.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1906\" data-end=\"1922\">Cavity Check</strong>: The dentist will examine the surfaces of your teeth for any signs of cavities or decay. They may use a small mirror and dental instruments to check for soft spots or holes in the teeth, which could indicate the presence of cavities.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2161\" data-end=\"2186\">Gum Health Evaluation</strong>: The dentist will assess the health of your gums by checking for signs of gingivitis (early gum disease) or periodontitis (advanced gum disease). This may involve measuring the depth of the gum pockets around each tooth. Healthy gums should be firm and not bleed when touched. If your gums are swollen, tender, or bleed easily, this may indicate a gum infection that requires treatment.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2578\" data-end=\"2610\">Occlusion (Bite) Examination</strong>: The dentist will check how your upper and lower teeth come together when you bite (occlusion). Misalignment or uneven wear on the teeth can lead to jaw pain, tooth grinding, and other dental issues. If necessary, the dentist may recommend orthodontic treatment to correct bite problems.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2903\" data-end=\"2928\">Oral Cancer Screening</strong>: During the checkup, the dentist will screen for signs of oral cancer. This includes checking for unusual lumps or lesions in the mouth, throat, and neck, as well as any changes in the tissues of the tongue, gums, and soft palate. Early detection of oral cancer can improve treatment outcomes.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"3227\" data-end=\"3249\">Fluoride Treatment</strong>: Depending on your oral health, the dentist may apply a fluoride treatment to help protect your teeth from cavities and strengthen enamel. This is especially beneficial for individuals who are at higher risk of tooth decay or those with sensitive teeth.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"3509\" data-end=\"3538\">Treatment Recommendations</strong>: After completing the examination, the dentist will discuss any issues discovered during the checkup. They will recommend appropriate treatments, which may include fillings, deep cleaning for gum disease, fluoride treatments, or referrals to specialists (such as an orthodontist or periodontist). If no issues are found, the dentist will provide recommendations for maintaining healthy teeth and gums, including proper brushing and flossing techniques.\n<br /></li></ol>\r\n<div>A <span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Dental Checkup</span> is a critical component of maintaining good oral health. It not only helps prevent common dental problems, such as cavities and gum disease, but it also enables early detection of more serious conditions, including oral cancer. Regular checkups are recommended every six months to ensure your teeth and gums remain healthy and to address any potential issues before they escalate. A healthy smile contributes to overall health and well-being, making dental checkups a vital part of your healthcare routine.\n<br /></div>', 1, 10, '2025-04-18 18:11:29', '2025-04-18 18:11:29');
INSERT INTO `services` (`id`, `name`, `thumbnail`, `duration`, `price`, `description`, `clinic_id`, `speciality_id`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(13, 'Diabetes Screening', 'clinic_image_68029634c6260.webp', 60, 75, '<p>\n\n<p data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"508\"><strong data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"22\">Diabetes Screening</strong> is a medical process used to identify individuals at risk for diabetes or those who may have the condition but are not yet showing symptoms. Diabetes, particularly type 2 diabetes, can develop gradually, and many individuals may not realize they have the condition until it causes serious complications. Screening helps detect the disease early, enabling timely interventions that can prevent or delay the onset of complications such as heart disease, kidney failure, and nerve damage.</p><p data-start=\"0\" data-end=\"508\" style=\"text-align: center;\">\n\n<img src=\"http://127.0.0.1:8000/storage/ServiceContent/service_content_680296d3dbcbb.webp\" style=\" text-align: center; max-width: 80%\" />\n<br /></p>\n<p data-start=\"510\" data-end=\"705\">During a <strong data-start=\"519\" data-end=\"541\">Diabetes Screening</strong>, several key tests and evaluations are used to determine the presence of diabetes or prediabetes. The primary components of a diabetes screening typically include:</p>\n<ol><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"710\" data-end=\"736\">Medical History Review</strong>: The healthcare provider will start by asking about your family history of diabetes, personal history of conditions such as high blood pressure or high cholesterol, lifestyle factors (such as diet and physical activity), and any symptoms you may be experiencing, such as excessive thirst, frequent urination, unexplained weight loss, or fatigue.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1087\" data-end=\"1111\">Physical Examination</strong>: The doctor will perform a physical examination, measuring vital signs like blood pressure and checking for signs of complications related to diabetes, such as nerve damage, poor circulation, or changes in the skin or eyes.\n<br /></li><li><span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Fasting Blood Glucose Test</span>: This test measures your blood sugar level after fasting for at least 8 hours. It is one of the most common methods for diagnosing diabetes or prediabetes.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"1778\" data-end=\"1816\">Oral Glucose Tolerance Test (OGTT)</strong>: This test is often used to diagnose gestational diabetes (during pregnancy) or type 2 diabetes. It involves drinking a sugary solution, and then blood glucose levels are measured at regular intervals (usually after 1 hour and 2 hours). It checks how well your body processes sugar over time.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2291\" data-end=\"2314\">Hemoglobin A1c Test</strong>: This blood test measures your average blood glucose levels over the past 2-3 months. It is used to diagnose both diabetes and prediabetes.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2633\" data-end=\"2662\">Random Blood Glucose Test</strong>: This test measures blood sugar levels at any given time, regardless of when you last ate. A random blood glucose level of 200 mg/dL or higher, along with symptoms of diabetes (such as excessive thirst or frequent urination), may indicate diabetes.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"2916\" data-end=\"2949\">Cholesterol and Lipid Profile</strong>: Since individuals with diabetes are at a higher risk of heart disease, a lipid profile test is often included to assess cholesterol levels, including total cholesterol, LDL (bad cholesterol), HDL (good cholesterol), and triglycerides. High cholesterol levels can indicate an increased risk of cardiovascular issues associated with diabetes.\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"3296\" data-end=\"3321\">Kidney Function Tests</strong>: Diabetes can affect kidney function over time. Tests such as urine tests for protein (microalbuminuria) or blood tests for creatinine and glomerular filtration rate (GFR) help assess kidney health and detect any early signs of diabetic nephropathy (kidney disease).\n<br /></li><li>\n\n<strong data-start=\"3593\" data-end=\"3623\">Blood Pressure Measurement</strong>: Since individuals with diabetes are also at higher risk for high blood pressure, blood pressure monitoring is part of the screening. Managing blood pressure is essential in diabetes care to reduce the risk of heart disease and other complications.\n<br /></li></ol><div>\n\n<strong data-start=\"4148\" data-end=\"4170\">Diabetes Screening</strong> is crucial for identifying the risk of diabetes or diagnosing it early when the condition is easier to manage. Early intervention, including lifestyle changes (such as diet and exercise) and, in some cases, medication, can help prevent or delay the onset of diabetes complications. Screening is especially important for individuals over the age of 45, those with a family history of diabetes, people who are overweight, or those who have a history of gestational diabetes. Regular screening and monitoring can significantly improve long-term health outcomes and prevent the complications associated with diabetes.\n<br /></div></p>', 1, 6, '2025-04-18 18:16:03', '2025-04-18 18:16:03');
